VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pause the board and move on to the landscape. For the past few weeks, Alicia has been drawing illustrations for the landscape using a configuration we developed for the punchboard. By the way, punchboard is, you know,'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you, well, everyone watching this has bought a board game at some point. You know, when you get a big square of cardboard and push chips out of it, it\'s really satisfying. This is called a punchboard'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. And the reason it\'s done this way is so you can create one layout with all the individual elements, and the machines can cut out all the shapes with tiny jumpers so people can extrude them. This'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simplifies the process so you don\'t have to make all the small parts separately. You just make one big sheet that becomes all the little details. So we go through the process of creating designs that work and become punchboards. And'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there are production constraints. One such limitation in this case is that we will have one board that has to meet all of our landscaping needs. That\'s why we\'re making this board double-sided: on one side, all the'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'landscape elements will be convex, and if you flip them over, the same details will become an abyss. So, taking Alicia\'s final punchboard layout and the vector template itself, I'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'laser cut the punchboard— actually the same layout we\'ll be using. I thought I could just UV print on top of that and flatten it out so I'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
